// Thread: profiles and standard sizes
//
// One place for what a screw thread IS, so a bore and the fastener that goes
// into it are drawn from the same definition rather than from two copies.
//
// The form follows ISO 68-1. A basic triangle of height H = (sqrt3/2) * P sits
// on a base of one pitch, and the thread is that triangle truncated at both
// ends — H/8 off the apex and H/4 off the base — leaving a form (5/8)H deep
// with 60-degree flanks, a flat P/8 wide at the major diameter and a flat P/4
// wide at the minor.
//
// THAT IS ONE PROFILE, AND BOTH THREADS ARE IT. The external thread is the
// metal inside it and the internal thread is the metal outside it, so at basic
// size male() and female() are the same field read from opposite sides — which
// is exactly why a screw goes into the hole tapped for it, with its flanks in
// contact and nothing to spare. Real clearance comes from the tolerance class
// (a fundamental deviation on the external thread) and from the tap drill,
// which is wider than the basic minor diameter and truncates the nut's crest;
// list a blind() under a tap and the void picks that truncation up on its own.
//
// THE FIELD IS A BOOLEAN. A thread is a cylinder at the major diameter with a
// helical groove taken out of it, and that groove is CONVEX — above the minor
// diameter, inside a 60-degree V — so it is an intersection of half-planes.
// max and min the whole way down, which is what keeps the gradient at one by
// construction rather than by measurement, where a radial difference gives ~2
// across a 60-degree flank and has to be scaled to hide it.
//
// The one correction is the lead angle, which stretches the axial coordinate
// when the helix is folded onto one turn. It rides on the FLANK alone, since
// the cylinder has no lead: 0.9984 there, and 1.0 exactly on the crest.
//
// Prove against the smooth forms anyway. Unit gradient or not, the helix is
// fine, so an interval proof against a thread subdivides to thread scale to
// tell you what the plain cylinder already did. drill.tap and the fastener
// smooth forms exist for that, at maximum material, and the Engagement zones
// below say which part of their overlap is the thread.
//
// THE THREAD DATUM is phase 0 at z = 0 on +x, where the profile is at its
// MAJOR diameter: a screw carries a crest there and the hole tapped for it
// carries a root. Everything in this package shares it, including stripe().
//
// THE METRIC SIZES LIVE IN mechanical/catalog, one row per size shared with
// the fastener dimensions for that size, so a pitch and a screw head cannot
// disagree about which sizes exist. Only the inch series is tabulated here,
// because no inch fastener shares it.
//
// Doc convention follows builtin/base.gsl: `///` lines are public docs. The
// size table below is data, and is left undocumented on purpose.

// =========================================================
// Size Tables (internal)
// =========================================================

// The METRIC sizes are not here. They live in one row per size in
// mechanical/catalog, alongside the fastener dimensions for the same size, so
// that a pitch and a screw head cannot disagree about which sizes exist. This
// package used to carry M16, M20 and M24 pitches that fastener had no rows
// for, which is exactly the failure that arrangement prevents.
//
// The INCH sizes stay here. The one inch fastener the catalog carries, ASME
// B18.6.3's square-drive screw, keys its own tables by the same inch nominal
// and reads its pitch from this one; inch socket screws (ASME B18.3) are still
// absent, and inventing them alongside an ISO library would be worse than their
// absence. One table, both series, keyed by
// nominal diameter in INCHES; the pitches are in millimetres, as 25.4 divided
// by threads per inch.
//
//    0  nominal diameter, inches
//    1  ASME B1.1 Unified Coarse pitch, mm
//    2  ASME B1.1 Unified Fine pitch, mm

inchTable = [[0.1120, 0.63500, 0.52917],
             [0.1380, 0.79375, 0.63500],
             [0.1640, 0.79375, 0.63500],
             [0.1900, 1.05833, 0.79375],
             [0.2500, 1.27000, 0.90714],
             [0.3125, 1.41111, 1.05833],
             [0.3750, 1.58750, 1.05833],
             [0.4375, 1.81429, 1.27000],
             [0.5000, 1.95385, 1.27000],
             [0.6250, 2.30909, 1.41111],
             [0.7500, 2.54000, 1.58750]];

// The inch accessors below use catalog.onRow with the same 0.0001 tolerance the
// metric ones do: it is float slack rather than a blend width, so it does not
// scale with the series. The inch rows run as close as 0.026 apart, which
// leaves it two orders of magnitude clear of blending two sizes together.
// It is a literal at each call for the reason catalog.onRow gives: a
// package-level constant referenced from a library body is lifted into a
// parameter of every remap the body is inlined into, which cost 126 KB of
// shader on one example.

// =========================================================
// Standard Sizes
// =========================================================

/// The coarse pitch of an ISO metric thread of the given nominal diameter in
/// millimetres: thread.metricPitch(6.0) is 1.0, the pitch of an M6. A diameter
/// that is not a catalog size returns 0 — threads exist only in standard
/// sizes, and a zero pitch will not build a thread.
///
/// ISO 261 first and second choice, M1 through M64, from mechanical/catalog.
metricPitch(nominal) {
  return catalog.pitch(nominal);
}

/// The first ISO 261 FINE pitch of an ISO metric thread of the given nominal
/// diameter in millimetres: thread.metricFinePitch(10.0) is 1.25, so an
/// M10 x 1.25. Zero where the catalog carries no fine pitch for the size.
///
/// A size has one coarse pitch and several fine ones, and only the first is
/// tabulated. Nothing forces you through the table: every thread function
/// below takes the pitch outright, so thread.male(10.0, 1.0) builds an M10 x 1
/// whether or not it is a catalog default.
metricFinePitch(nominal) {
  return catalog.finePitch(nominal);
}

/// The pitch in millimetres of a Unified Coarse thread of the given nominal
/// diameter in INCHES: thread.uncPitch(0.25) is the 20-per-inch pitch of a
/// 1/4-20, as 1.27 mm.
uncPitch(nominalInch) {
  k = nominalInch;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[1] * catalog.onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    inchTable));
}

/// The pitch in millimetres of a Unified Fine thread, nominal diameter in
/// INCHES: thread.unfPitch(0.25) is a 1/4-28.
unfPitch(nominalInch) {
  k = nominalInch;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[2] * catalog.onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    inchTable));
}

/// The basic minor diameter of an internal thread — the crest of the nut's
/// own material, and the smallest thing a screw of this size passes.
minorDia(major, pitchVal) {
  return major - 2.0 * formDepth(pitchVal);
}

/// The basic pitch diameter, where the flanks are half a pitch apart. The
/// diameter a thread is actually toleranced on.
pitchDia(major, pitchVal) {
  return major - 0.6495 * pitchVal;
}

/// The drill to run before tapping: nominal minus pitch, the shop rule that
/// lands near 75 percent thread engagement. thread.tapDrill(6.0, 1.0) is 5.0,
/// the drill for an M6.
tapDrill(major, pitchVal) {
  return major - pitchVal;
}

// =========================================================
// Profile (internal)
// =========================================================

// formDepth: (5/8)H, the radial depth of the truncated form, for H = (sqrt3/2)P.
formDepth(pitchVal) {
  return 0.5412659 * pitchVal;
}

// helixPhase: a triangle wave in the helical phase, 0 on the major-diameter
// plane and 1 on the minor, one period per pitch. acos of the helical cosine
// is that triangle directly; the cosine is expanded through x/r and y/r, so no
// atan is needed and every branch is finite under intervals.
helixPhase(pitchVal) {
  radial = max(length(p.xy), 0.2);
  ang = 2.0 * pi * p.z / max(pitchVal, 0.01);
  along = cos(ang) * (p.x / radial) + sin(ang) * (p.y / radial);
  return acos(clamp(along, -1.0, 1.0)) / pi;
}

// rootOffset: the axial distance, in millimetres, from the nearest
// MINOR-diameter plane — the middle of the groove. The groove is symmetric
// about that plane, which is what makes folding onto it exact rather than
// merely close: a nearest point in the neighbouring turn reflects into this
// one without ever moving further away.
rootOffset(pitchVal) {
  return (1.0 - helixPhase(pitchVal)) * pitchVal * 0.5;
}

// leadCos: the cosine of the lead angle — how much the fold stretches the
// axial coordinate. A millimetre along the axis is 1/cos(lead) of folded
// offset, because the phase turns with the angle too.
//
// Read 0.65 of a pitch BELOW the minor diameter, not at it. The lead angle
// grows as the radius falls, so the reference has to be the smallest radius
// where the flank half-plane can still be the nearer one — and that is where
// it gives way to the groove floor. Setting the two equal at the crest plane,
// 0.866*(0.375P + d*tan30) = d, puts the crossover at d = 0.646P, so 0.65P
// covers it for every size. Read at the minor diameter instead and the field
// creeps to 1.0008 down in the core; read here and it never exceeds one.
leadCos(major, pitchVal) {
  reference = max(major / 2.0 - formDepth(pitchVal) - 0.65 * pitchVal, 0.01);
  return reference / length([reference, pitchVal / (2.0 * pi)]);
}

// flankScale: what brings the flank half-plane's gradient back to one.
//
// Its normal is cos30 along the folded axial coordinate and sin30 along the
// radius, and ONLY the axial part is stretched by the fold, so the factor is
// 1/sqrt((cos30/leadCos)^2 + sin30^2) rather than leadCos itself: 0.9984 for
// an M6, against the 0.9979 that scaling a whole field costs. The difference
// is the point. A correction belongs on the one term that earns it, and the
// cylinder the groove is cut from earns none at all — a cylinder has no lead,
// so it keeps its exact distance.
flankScale(major, pitchVal) {
  axial = 0.8660254 / leadCos(major, pitchVal);
  return 1.0 / length([axial, 0.5]);
}

// groove: the helical V the thread is cut with — every point above the minor
// diameter and inside a 60-degree V opening outward from the root.
//
// THAT REGION IS CONVEX, so it is an intersection of half-planes and nothing
// else: a max, with no magnitude and no sign anywhere in it. The 0.8660254 is
// the flank plane's own unit normal (its gradient is 2/sqrt(3)), so it is a
// normalization and not a correction.
//
// The other half of the V is unreachable: rootOffset is folded, so the axial
// offset is never negative and the opposite flank can never be the nearer one.
groove(major, pitchVal) {
  minorR = major / 2.0 - formDepth(pitchVal);
  rad = length(p.xy);
  flank = rootOffset(pitchVal) - pitchVal / 8.0 - (rad - minorR) * 0.57735027;
  return max(minorR - rad, flank * 0.8660254 * flankScale(major, pitchVal));
}

// basicForm: the ISO 68-1 basic profile as a field, negative inside the
// external thread's metal, which is the same region as an internal thread's
// void. Unbounded along z.
//
// A boolean, start to finish: the cylinder at the major diameter, less the
// groove. Nothing here is a magnitude multiplied by a sign.
basicForm(major, pitchVal) {
  return max(length(p.xy) - major / 2.0, neg(groove(major, pitchVal)));
}

// sinForm: the same envelope as a sinusoid instead of a truncated V. One
// function for both hands, so a sinusoidal screw meshes with a sinusoidal
// tapped hole rather than clashing with it half a pitch out of step.
sinForm(major, pitchVal) {
  radial = max(length(p.xy), 0.2);
  ang = 2.0 * pi * p.z / max(pitchVal, 0.01);
  along = cos(ang) * (p.x / radial) + sin(ang) * (p.y / radial);
  radiusAt = major / 2.0 - formDepth(pitchVal) * 0.5 * (1.0 - along);
  return (length(p.xy) - radiusAt) * 0.7;
}

// =========================================================
// Thread Profiles
// =========================================================

/// The surface of an EXTERNAL thread of the given major diameter and pitch —
/// the screw itself, negative inside the metal. The ISO 68-1 basic profile,
/// unbounded along z: bound it where you use it.
///
/// A true distance field, so it traces and meshes like any other solid. Prove
/// against the smooth cylinder at the major diameter all the same: a proof
/// against the helix subdivides to thread scale for nothing the cylinder does
/// not already give you.
male(major, pitchVal) {
  return basicForm(major, pitchVal);
}

/// The void an INTERNAL thread presents — a tapped hole, negative inside the
/// hole. Unbounded along z.
///
/// The SAME field as male() at the same size, and that is not an oversight:
/// one basic profile separates the two threads, so a screw and the hole tapped
/// for it meet flank to flank with nothing between them. What opens the fit in
/// a real joint is the tolerance class and the tap drill — and the drill you
/// list under a tap() already cuts the nut's crest back to itself.
female(major, pitchVal) {
  return basicForm(major, pitchVal);
}

/// An external thread as a SINUSOID rather than a truncated V. Not a standard
/// form and no longer cheaper to bound, now that the real one is a distance;
/// what it still buys is a field with no corners in it. Reach for it when the
/// thread is scenery.
maleSin(major, pitchVal) {
  return sinForm(major, pitchVal);
}

/// The sinusoidal internal form, the counterpart of maleSin — the same field,
/// for the same reason female() is male().
femaleSin(major, pitchVal) {
  return sinForm(major, pitchVal);
}

/// A thread that costs no geometry: 0 on the major diameter and 1 on the
/// minor, running helically at the given pitch. Paint it onto a SMOOTH bore or
/// shank and the thread reads at any zoom, while the mesher still sees a
/// cylinder and a proof still runs against the true envelope.
///
///   shank = fastener.shcs(6.0, 20.0);
///   shank.color = color.mixOklab(steel, dark, thread.stripe(6.0, 1.0));
stripe(major, pitchVal) {
  return helixPhase(pitchVal);
}

// =========================================================
// Relief
// =========================================================
//
// A THREAD IS A MACHINING OPERATION, and these draw it as one. The part is
// built at its MAXIMUM MATERIAL CONDITION — a screw's shank at the major
// diameter, a tapped hole's bore at the minor — and threading only ever takes
// material away from that. So the geometry is what the mesher meshes, what an
// export carries and what a proof runs against, and the thread is a height
// handed to material.withDisplacement, which the renderer alone reads:
//
//   screw = fastener.shcs(6.0, 20.0);
//   steelMat, steelColor = metal.steel();
//   screw.material = material.withDisplacement(steelMat,
//                        thread.maleRelief(6.0, 1.0, 0.0, -20.0));
//
// A screw and the hole tapped for it therefore INTERFERE AS DESIGNED, by the
// annulus between the two diameters over the length they share. That overlap
// is the thread engagement, and the relief is what resolves it on screen.
//
// NOTHING HERE IS APPROXIMATE ON THE CYLINDER. A thread's surface is a radial
// height over its helical phase, r = major/2 - depth, so subtracting that
// height from a cylinder draws exactly the ISO 68-1 profile male() and
// female() are distances to. What it is not is a distance: across a flank the
// displaced field runs at about twice the rate of the true one, which the
// renderer's march divides out and which is why this is for display and never
// for a proof.
//
// EVERY RELIEF IS BOUNDED, because a height rides the surface normal of
// whatever face it is handed to, and a helical height on a screw head or a
// shank's end face is not a thread. The bounds are runout(): the depth a relief
// may reach, zero on each bound and rising half as fast as the distance inside
// it, so the thread runs out over a pitch or so the way a rolled one does and
// the whole part can wear one material. The half is load-bearing. At a rate of
// one the height cancels the end face's own distance and leaves a slab of
// exact zero a form depth thick — the membrane drill.engrave warns about,
// arrived at a third way.
//
// AN EXTERNAL THREAD IS ALSO BOUNDED INWARD, toward the axis, or a section cut
// through it shows the helix pressed into the whole cut face: the height rides
// that face's normal too, and the shank's axis is as much inside the thread's
// reach as its crest is. So the male relief fades to nothing a form depth
// under the root. Toward the axis it may rise at the full rate, because there
// the shank's own distance and the height grow TOGETHER — nothing cancels —
// and at a rate of one the bound is a full form depth at the root, exactly
// what the root takes, so the cylinder renders as before. A section still shows
// a band two form depths deep dented by the helix rather than the thread's true
// notch; a height cannot draw a hole in a face, only push it back.

// profileDepth: the radial depth of the basic profile below the major
// diameter at this point's helical phase — nothing across the crest flat, the
// full form depth across the root flat, and the 60-degree flank between, whose
// radius falls sqrt3 for every millimetre it runs along the axis.
profileDepth(pitchVal) {
  offset = helixPhase(pitchVal) * pitchVal * 0.5;
  return clamp((offset - pitchVal / 16.0) * 1.7320508, 0.0, formDepth(pitchVal));
}

// runout: the most a relief may take away here — zero on and beyond the planes
// z = zTop and z = zBottom and the cylinder of radius outerR, and rising at half
// the distance inside all three. See the Relief note above for why half.
runout(zTop, zBottom, outerR) {
  return max(0.0, 0.5 * min(zTop - p.z, p.z - zBottom, outerR - length(p.xy)));
}

/// The EXTERNAL thread as a displacement height, in millimetres, for
/// material.withDisplacement: at most zero, the depth threading takes off a
/// smooth shank at the `major` diameter, between the planes z = `zTop` and
/// z = `zBottom`. Hand it to the shank the smooth fastener forms build, and
/// the screw meshes and proves as its major-diameter envelope while it renders
/// with the ISO 68-1 thread.
///
///   screw = fastener.shcs(6.0, 20.0);
///   steelMat, steelColor = metal.steel();
///   screw.material = material.withDisplacement(steelMat,
///                        thread.maleRelief(6.0, 1.0, 0.0, -20.0));
///
/// `zTop` is where the thread stops under the head — the seating plane z = 0
/// for a cap screw, the bottom of the cone for a countersunk one — and
/// `zBottom` is the tip. The thread runs out over about a pitch inside each,
/// and nothing beyond a form depth outside the major diameter is touched, so
/// the head keeps its own surface whichever material it wears.
maleRelief(major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom) {
  outerR = major / 2.0 + formDepth(pitchVal);
  innerR = major / 2.0 - 2.0 * formDepth(pitchVal);
  return neg(min(profileDepth(pitchVal), runout(zTop, zBottom, outerR),
                 max(0.0, length(p.xy) - innerR)));
}

/// The INTERNAL thread as a displacement height: at most zero, the depth a tap
/// takes out of the wall of a bore `boreDia` across, between the planes
/// z = `zTop` (the mouth) and z = `zBottom`. Hand it to the PART, which carries
/// the plain bore.
///
/// Wherever the bore is wider than the thread's own crest the tap cuts nothing,
/// so a hole drilled at thread.tapDrill comes out with its crests truncated to
/// the drill, which is how a tapped hole is actually made. `boreDia` above the
/// major diameter leaves no thread at all.
femaleRelief(major, pitchVal, boreDia, zTop, zBottom) {
  majorR = major / 2.0;
  reach = majorR - boreDia / 2.0;
  cut = max(reach - profileDepth(pitchVal), 0.0);
  return neg(min(cut, runout(zTop, zBottom, majorR + 2.0 * reach)));
}

/// The internal thread relief on a bore at the BASIC MINOR diameter — the
/// maximum material condition of a tapped hole, and the smallest bore the
/// thread's own profile allows. Drill the bore with thread.minorDia(major,
/// pitch) to match.
femaleRelief(major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom) {
  return femaleRelief(major, pitchVal, minorDia(major, pitchVal), zTop, zBottom);
}

// sinDepth: the radial depth of the SINUSOIDAL form below the major diameter
// at this point's helical phase — sinForm's own depth, nothing on the crest and
// the full form depth half a pitch along, with no corner anywhere between.
sinDepth(pitchVal) {
  radial = max(length(p.xy), 0.2);
  ang = 2.0 * pi * p.z / max(pitchVal, 0.01);
  along = cos(ang) * (p.x / radial) + sin(ang) * (p.y / radial);
  return formDepth(pitchVal) * 0.5 * (1.0 - clamp(along, -1.0, 1.0));
}

/// The external thread as a COSMETIC SINUSOID, for material.withDisplacement:
/// maleRelief with maleSin's profile in place of the ISO one, and the same
/// arguments and bounds. Not a standard form. What it buys is a height with no
/// corners, which stays smooth where the V's flanks and flats would alias —
/// threads a few pixels a pitch, a board of small screws.
///
///   steelMat, steelColor = metal.steel();
///   screw.material = material.withDisplacement(steelMat,
///                        thread.maleSinRelief(6.0, 1.0, 0.0, -20.0));
maleSinRelief(major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom) {
  outerR = major / 2.0 + formDepth(pitchVal);
  innerR = major / 2.0 - 2.0 * formDepth(pitchVal);
  return neg(min(sinDepth(pitchVal), runout(zTop, zBottom, outerR),
                 max(0.0, length(p.xy) - innerR)));
}

/// The internal thread as a cosmetic sinusoid: femaleRelief with femaleSin's
/// profile, the same arguments and bounds, on a bore `boreDia` across. A
/// sinusoidal screw in a sinusoidal hole meshes, as maleSin and femaleSin do.
femaleSinRelief(major, pitchVal, boreDia, zTop, zBottom) {
  majorR = major / 2.0;
  reach = majorR - boreDia / 2.0;
  cut = max(reach - sinDepth(pitchVal), 0.0);
  return neg(min(cut, runout(zTop, zBottom, majorR + 2.0 * reach)));
}

/// The sinusoidal internal relief on a bore at the BASIC MINOR diameter.
femaleSinRelief(major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom) {
  return femaleSinRelief(major, pitchVal, minorDia(major, pitchVal), zTop, zBottom);
}

/// The colour a relief stands in as wherever it is not drawn as geometry:
/// `base` on the crest, darkening toward the root by the depth `height` (any
/// relief above, at the same `pitchVal`) takes off there. It fades to plain
/// `base` by displacementDrawn(), so where the renderer moves the surface the
/// thread is shape alone, and where the Detail slider has taken the
/// displacement from a part small on screen — or nothing displaces at all, as
/// in a mesh or an export — the same thread is paint. Too small to resolve, it
/// is `base`.
///
///   h = thread.maleRelief(6.0, 1.0, 0.0, -20.0);
///   steelMat, steelColor = metal.steel();
///   screw.material = material.withDisplacement(steelMat, h);
///   screw.color = thread.reliefColor(steelColor, h, 1.0);
reliefColor(base, height, pitchVal) {
  depth = clamp(neg(height) / formDepth(pitchVal), 0.0, 1.0);
  painted = color.mixOklab(base, base * 0.35, depth * (1.0 - displacementDrawn()));
  painted.simple = base;
  return painted;
}

/// The thread as PAINT alone: reliefColor's colour at full strength wherever
/// it is, for a part with no relief to draw the thread with — a smooth part
/// painted rather than displaced, or a fastener's simpler stand-in.
/// reliefColor cannot stand in for it there: displacementDrawn() is how much
/// displacement the renderer draws at a hit, whether or not the part has any,
/// so on a part close up it would take the paint away with nothing in its
/// place. Too small to resolve, it is `base`.
///
///   h = thread.maleRelief(6.0, 1.0, 0.0, -20.0);
///   steelMat, steelColor = metal.steel();
///   screw.color = thread.paint(steelColor, h, 1.0);
paint(base, height, pitchVal) {
  depth = clamp(neg(height) / formDepth(pitchVal), 0.0, 1.0);
  painted = color.mixOklab(base, base * 0.35, depth);
  painted.simple = base;
  return painted;
}

// =========================================================
// Engagement
// =========================================================
//
// A screw and its tapped hole interfere as designed — see Relief — and a
// proof of the joint has to say WHICH interference is designed. It is the
// part of the overlap both threads machine away: where the screw's thread and
// the hole's thread are both cut, the overlap is the engagement; anywhere
// else it is a collision. A plain shank in a tapped bore, a thread in a
// plain hole, an M8 screw in an M6 hole — each overlaps outside one of the
// two threads, so each fails.
//
// So each thread has a ZONE, the band its relief works in, taking the same
// arguments as the relief it goes with; the joint's zone is where the two
// intersect; and a fit is proven as
//
//   certify max(screw, plate, thread.engaged(screwZone, plateZone, margin)) >= 0
//
// with each zone remapped exactly as its part is. Whether the two threads
// are the same size, pitch and hand is a yes-or-no about two parts, not a
// field, and is not proven here.
//
// THE MARGIN IS NOT OPTIONAL. The overlap of the two smooth parts is exactly
// the annulus between the bore and the major diameter, so its walls ARE the
// zone's walls, and an exact zone leaves the proof at zero along two whole
// cylinders — the one thing interval arithmetic cannot settle. Growing the
// zone by `margin` lifts the field to margin/2 there, and the proof then
// resolves at cells of about the margin. The cost is that an overlap reaching
// less than `margin` outside the zone passes too: a tenth of a millimetre is
// under the major-diameter tolerance of an M6 6g thread.

/// The ZONE an EXTERNAL thread's relief works in, as a distance field negative
/// inside: the band a form depth deep under the `major` diameter, between the
/// planes z = `zTop` and z = `zBottom`. Same arguments as maleRelief.
maleZone(major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom) {
  majorR = major / 2.0;
  return max(length(p.xy) - majorR, (majorR - formDepth(pitchVal)) - length(p.xy),
             p.z - zTop, zBottom - p.z);
}

/// The ZONE an INTERNAL thread's relief works in: the band from the wall of a
/// bore `boreDia` across out to the `major` diameter, between the planes
/// z = `zTop` and z = `zBottom`. Same arguments as femaleRelief; the pitch
/// changes nothing here and is taken so the two calls read alike.
femaleZone(major, pitchVal, boreDia, zTop, zBottom) {
  return max(length(p.xy) - major / 2.0, boreDia / 2.0 - length(p.xy),
             p.z - zTop, zBottom - p.z);
}

/// The internal thread's zone on a bore at the BASIC MINOR diameter — the one
/// drill.tap and fastener.nut cut.
femaleZone(major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom) {
  return femaleZone(major, pitchVal, minorDia(major, pitchVal), zTop, zBottom);
}

/// Where a threaded joint MAY overlap, as a field POSITIVE there: inside both
/// threads' zones, grown by `margin`. Take the max of the two parts with it
/// and prove the result is not negative — it is below zero only where the
/// parts overlap outside the engagement:
///
///   screw = fastener.shcs(6.0, 20.0);
///   plate = max(box([-10.0, -10.0, -12.0], [10.0, 10.0, 0.0]),
///               -drill.hole(drill.tap(6.0, 14.0), 0.0));
///   ok = max(screw, plate, thread.engaged(thread.maleZone(6.0, 1.0, 0.0, -20.0),
///                                         thread.femaleZone(6.0, 1.0, 0.0, -12.0), 0.1));
///
/// and certify `ok` over a region that stops just below the seating plane,
/// since the head resting on the plate is contact of its own. The tap runs
/// past the plate's far face: stopping on it leaves the bore's floor in that
/// face, a membrane no proof gets past. See the
/// Engagement note above for why the margin, and what it lets through.
engaged(zoneA, zoneB, margin) {
  return margin - max(zoneA, zoneB);
}

// =========================================================
// Tolerance Position (ISO 965-1)
// =========================================================
//
// A BASIC THREAD HAS NO CLEARANCE: male() and female() are one profile, so a
// screw at basic size meets the hole tapped for it flank to flank. The fit
// comes from the TOLERANCE POSITION, a fundamental deviation that moves the
// external thread's whole profile — major, pitch and minor diameter alike —
// down from basic, or the internal thread's up. An M6 6g screw in a 6H hole is
// the everyday fit: g on the screw, H, which is no deviation at all, on the
// hole.
//
// Apply one by moving the diameter a thread is built at. A 6g M6 is the shank,
// its relief and its zone all at
//
//   6.0 + thread.deviationG(1.0)        // 5.974
//
// and its flanks then stand |es|/2 off a basic hole's all the way round the
// helix, since both profiles are the same shape at the same phase.
//
// ISO 965-1 derives the deviations from formulas, in micrometres for P in
// millimetres — e −(50 + 11P), f −(30 + 11P), g −(15 + 11P), h 0, and G
// +(15 + 11P), H 0 — and then TABULATES them, rounded to the R 40 preferred
// numbers and then to whole micrometres, "not always" by that rule. The table
// is what the standard specifies, and it is what is returned here: it is off
// the formula by up to 5.5 µm for e (at P 0.5), 3 for g and G (at P 8) and
// 1.75 for f, so an M8's g is 28, not 28.75, and an M10's is 32, not 31.5.
//
// A DASH IN THE TABLE IS A ZERO HERE, and so is a pitch the table does not
// list — which reads as position h, so check the pitch. e starts at P 0.5 and
// f at P 0.35; below that the standard gives neither.
//
// REFERENCES: ISO 965-1 section 13.1 (the formulas) and Table 1, as BS ISO
// 965-1 (the 1998 text), https://regbar.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/ISO-965-1-1998-%C3%96NEML%C4%B0.pdf;
// ISO 965-1:2013 Table 1, the same G, H, e, f, g and h values beside its new a
// to d positions, https://cdn.standards.iteh.ai/samples/57778/765c17fc0f7946ff850db22b43920f09/ISO-965-1-2013.pdf.

// ISO 965-1 Table 1 in millimetres. Columns: [pitch P, G's EI (+, and g's es
// is the same number negated at every pitch), e's es, f's es].
deviation965 = [[0.2,  0.017,  0.0,    0.0],
                [0.25, 0.018,  0.0,    0.0],
                [0.3,  0.018,  0.0,    0.0],
                [0.35, 0.019,  0.0,   -0.034],
                [0.4,  0.019,  0.0,   -0.034],
                [0.45, 0.020,  0.0,   -0.035],
                [0.5,  0.020, -0.050, -0.036],
                [0.6,  0.021, -0.053, -0.036],
                [0.7,  0.022, -0.056, -0.038],
                [0.75, 0.022, -0.056, -0.038],
                [0.8,  0.024, -0.060, -0.038],
                [1.0,  0.026, -0.060, -0.040],
                [1.25, 0.028, -0.063, -0.042],
                [1.5,  0.032, -0.067, -0.045],
                [1.75, 0.034, -0.071, -0.048],
                [2.0,  0.038, -0.071, -0.052],
                [2.5,  0.042, -0.080, -0.058],
                [3.0,  0.048, -0.085, -0.063],
                [3.5,  0.053, -0.090, -0.070],
                [4.0,  0.060, -0.095, -0.075],
                [4.5,  0.063, -0.100, -0.080],
                [5.0,  0.071, -0.106, -0.085],
                [5.5,  0.075, -0.112, -0.090],
                [6.0,  0.080, -0.118, -0.095],
                [8.0,  0.100, -0.140, -0.118]];

/// The fundamental deviation es of an EXTERNAL thread in position e, in
/// millimetres and at most zero — the loosest of the four, for threads that
/// will be plated thick. ISO 965-1 Table 1; zero below P 0.5.
deviationE(pitchVal) {
  k = pitchVal;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[2] * catalog.onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    deviation965));
}

/// The fundamental deviation es of an external thread in position f. ISO 965-1
/// Table 1; zero below P 0.35.
deviationF(pitchVal) {
  k = pitchVal;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[3] * catalog.onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    deviation965));
}

/// The fundamental deviation es of an external thread in position g — the one
/// a 6g screw carries: thread.deviationG(1.0) is -0.026 for an M6.
deviationG(pitchVal) {
  k = pitchVal;
  return -reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                 map((r) { return r[1] * catalog.onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                     deviation965));
}

/// Position h: no deviation, the external thread at basic.
deviationH(pitchVal) {
  return 0.0;
}

/// The fundamental deviation EI of an INTERNAL thread in position G, in
/// millimetres and at least zero: the hole opened up by it.
internalDeviationG(pitchVal) {
  k = pitchVal;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[1] * catalog.onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    deviation965));
}

/// Position H: no deviation, the internal thread at basic — the everyday
/// tapped hole.
internalDeviationH(pitchVal) {
  return 0.0;
}
